WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.226
On 12 August 2026,
this valley in northern Nepal,

00:00:04.354 --> 00:00:06.920
high in the Himalaya, looks quiet.

00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:12.086
Fourteen days later, a dark swath
runs through the same valley.

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:16.591
For seven weeks before that, a radar
satellite had been picking up movement

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:19.203
in a mountain slope in northern Nepal.

00:00:20.016 --> 00:00:21.769
What had the satellites seen?

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.963
And what does it not prove?

00:00:24.474 --> 00:00:27.354
First, what officials
and news outlets said.

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:31.707
The US Geological Survey said
a rapid slope failure involving

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:37.002
a glacier struck Langtang National Park
in Nepal, near the Chinese border,

00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:40.577
on the morning of 26 August 2026.

00:00:41.181 --> 00:00:45.790
It said the debris flow and flood
traveled about 100 kilometers, roughly

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:51.549
62 miles, along two rivers,
the Lende Khola and the Trishuli Khola.

00:00:52.153 --> 00:00:55.368
It said the flood hit
populated areas downstream,

00:00:55.577 --> 00:00:58.236
including the Rasuwagadhi border post.

00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:02.462
Its summary does not say
where along that route the water stopped.

00:01:02.787 --> 00:01:05.260
The failure itself shook the ground.

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:08.941
USGS said it released seismic energy equal

00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:11.785
to a magnitude 5.2 earthquake.

00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:16.011
USGS said a second signal,
about three hours later,

00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:18.565
was equal to magnitude 4.2.

00:01:19.169 --> 00:01:22.315
USGS said those are energy comparisons:

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.613
the slope failure itself
produced the signals.

00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:31.174
USGS also said it has not yet determined
whether this began as a landslide

00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:35.504
that picked up part of a glacier,
or as a failure of the glacier itself.

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:39.196
And it said everything it
has published is preliminary.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:42.169
This mountain has done
something like this before.

00:01:42.772 --> 00:01:45.512
In 2015, USGS said,

00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:50.458
a magnitude 7.8 earthquake set off
a debris avalanche on the same mountain.

00:01:50.865 --> 00:01:55.079
USGS said it fell nearly 1,900
meters, buried a village,

00:01:55.218 --> 00:01:57.215
and killed more than 200 people.

00:01:57.981 --> 00:01:59.410
Now the human cost.

00:01:59.978 --> 00:02:05.481
In its 13 October release, ICIMOD reported
figures from Nepal's disaster authority:

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.519
1,451 people dead
and 5,745 missing in Nepal.

00:02:12.018 --> 00:02:16.546
Other lists differ slightly and keep
changing, so these numbers are not final.

00:02:16.778 --> 00:02:19.436
China counts its own side
of the border separately.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.568
Xinhua reported 43 dead
and 519 missing at Gyirong Port

00:02:24.812 --> 00:02:26.472
as of 5 September.

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:30.350
It was not clear whether some people
appear in both countries' counts,

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:32.498
so we do not add the two together.

00:02:32.881 --> 00:02:35.180
Satellites cannot see
people on the ground.

00:02:35.331 --> 00:02:40.706
They can see ground, ice, cloud,
and sometimes tiny movements of a slope.

00:02:41.089 --> 00:02:44.305
The mountain research
organization ICIMOD published

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.359
a preliminary analysis on 13 October.

00:02:47.637 --> 00:02:51.816
It said the disaster was not a conventional
glacial lake outburst flood.

00:02:52.142 --> 00:02:54.835
It said a failure
in the bedrock sheared away part

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.890
of the glacier lying on top of it.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.023
ICIMOD also said that radar
satellites, Sentinel-1,

00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:06.933
detected up to 40 centimeters of ground
movement between 7 July and 24 August.

00:03:07.316 --> 00:03:10.520
That is movement along the
satellite's line of sight,

00:03:10.694 --> 00:03:12.413
not the full motion of the slope.

00:03:12.796 --> 00:03:15.570
And the period ended two
days before the failure.

00:03:15.953 --> 00:03:19.274
We do not claim this was a warning
anyone could have acted on.

00:03:19.518 --> 00:03:21.468
No source we found shows that.

00:03:21.793 --> 00:03:24.057
ICIMOD's early estimate of the volume

00:03:24.092 --> 00:03:28.782
was about 132 million cubic
meters of rock and ice.

00:03:29.224 --> 00:03:32.881
Another early analysis suggested
a much smaller volume.

00:03:33.055 --> 00:03:37.153
So the size is not settled,
and we do not rely on one number.

00:03:37.664 --> 00:03:39.034
Now our own images.

00:03:39.208 --> 00:03:41.298
They come from free public data.

00:03:41.623 --> 00:03:44.525
First we looked for the clearest
view before the event.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.367
The best was 12 August, with 19
percent cloud cover over the scene.

00:03:49.692 --> 00:03:52.130
But between 14 and 27 August,

00:03:52.339 --> 00:03:57.482
every Sentinel-2 pass had between 39
and 97 percent cloud cover.

00:03:57.923 --> 00:04:00.048
Radar does not have that cloud problem,

00:04:00.222 --> 00:04:04.785
but the radar numbers in this video
are ICIMOD's, not ours.

00:04:05.028 --> 00:04:09.580
So for 26 August we used
a different satellite, Landsat 9.

00:04:09.963 --> 00:04:14.537
Its pixels are 30 meters wide,
three times coarser than Sentinel-2,

00:04:14.688 --> 00:04:17.358
and we show them as blocks,
without sharpening.

00:04:17.962 --> 00:04:22.061
In the 12 August image,
the river is a narrow pale line.

00:04:22.386 --> 00:04:27.749
In the 26 August image, a dark
grey-brown swath follows the same valley.

00:04:28.075 --> 00:04:31.766
From the image alone, we cannot
tell what the swath is made of.

00:04:32.045 --> 00:04:33.531
We traced it by eye.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.434
On our grid it runs about 30 kilometers.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:41.357
About 20 of them lie between the glacier
and the point where the river turns south,

00:04:41.601 --> 00:04:43.888
near the Rasuwagadhi border post.

00:04:44.329 --> 00:04:48.265
Clouds hide other stretches,
so this is the least we can follow,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:49.774
not the full length.

00:04:50.587 --> 00:04:54.523
USGS said the flow traveled
about 100 kilometers.

00:04:54.906 --> 00:04:56.984
Our frame cannot show all of it.

00:04:57.425 --> 00:05:01.512
USGS said it is mapping the full
extent by comparing satellite images

00:05:01.559 --> 00:05:03.114
from before and after.

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:06.353
Our trace is not a substitute
for that official map.

00:05:06.864 --> 00:05:08.269
What can we not say?

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.065
These images show what
the ground looked like on two days.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.643
They are not a measurement
of the flood itself.

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:17.510
We cannot say how fast the flow moved,

00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:20.459
or how high the water
rose in any one village.

00:05:20.784 --> 00:05:23.211
These images do not show that.

00:05:23.536 --> 00:05:28.145
We cannot say why the slope failed,
because that work is not finished.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.524
We cannot say anyone
could have been warned in time.

00:05:31.768 --> 00:05:36.272
And we cannot say how many people died,
because the lists are not final.

00:05:36.714 --> 00:05:38.815
Two things will settle more of this.

00:05:39.058 --> 00:05:42.251
The final findings from USGS and ICIMOD,

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:46.117
on the type and size
of the failure, are not out yet.

00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:48.300
And the official counts
of the dead and missing,

00:05:48.474 --> 00:05:51.376
on both sides of the border,
are not final.

00:05:51.887 --> 00:05:56.833
Every satellite frame here comes from free
public data, with the credits on screen.

00:05:57.077 --> 00:05:58.842
The sources are in the description.
